# One line ending in the working tree, on every machine: LF.
#
# Not a style preference — a correctness one. Several gates compare generated
# text against committed bytes:
#
#   * `vyrn doc --std --verify` diffs what the doc generator emits (LF) against
#     `docs/api/*.md` as checked out.
#   * `vyrn fmt --check` diffs the formatter's output against the `.vyrn` file.
#   * the release archive ships `std/` and `web/` verbatim, so a CRLF checkout
#     would publish CRLF sources.
#   * `curl … | sh` feeds `install.sh` straight to /bin/sh on Linux and macOS. A
#     CRLF committed from a Windows checkout would break the installer for
#     everyone, and break it invisibly on the machine that committed it.
#
# With `core.autocrlf=true` — Git for Windows' installer default — every one of
# those reads CRLF on Windows and LF on CI, so the same commit passes on one and
# fails on the other. `eol=lf` takes the decision away from a per-machine config.
#
# `text=auto` still lets Git detect binaries (the one `.png`) and leave them
# alone. Nothing in this repo needs CRLF: there are no `.bat`/`.cmd` files, and
# PowerShell reads LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# A pin is data a gate writes, so a merge never stops on one. `merge=pin` keeps
# the current side; the gate then fails on the stale number, and `VYRN_PIN=write`
# or `VYRN_WASM_MANIFEST=write` writes the right one. The driver is set once per
# clone: `git config merge.pin.driver true`.
compiler/vyrn-cli/tests/pins/** merge=pin
rfcs/census/wasm-sha256.tsv merge=pin
